# Counterexamples found in development (a review finding, a probe, a caught mutant; each is added in the commit of its fix):
# each case is the input that told the right result from a wrong one, with the expected
# result derived by hand from IEEE 1800, the clause, the classification and the origin
# (the `clause`, `class` and `origin` keys of every case).

== compact_window_discards_an_obligation_after_its_first_match
# 16.7 (sequence match), 16.12.7 (overlapping implication).
# The sole trigger at sample 0
# matches b at sample 1, discharging that obligation. Keeping its successors can
# incorrectly fail it at sample 3. Later attempts are vacuous because a is low.
property: a |-> ##[1:3] b
clause: 16.7 16.12.7
class: normative
origin: discharge mutant required by the plan
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1 2 3
a       : 1 . . .
b       : . 1 . .
outcome : T t t t
decided : 1 1 2 3
fail    : . . . .
pass    : . 1 . .
pending: 0

== isunknown_vacuous_operand_does_not_stop_and_from_covering
# 16.14.8 (p and q is nonvacuous when either operand is), 20.9. The verdict rests on the
# two-state model ($isunknown is 0), hence implementation-defined.
# The cover was skipped as "can never succeed".
# $isunknown(c) is 0, so ($isunknown(c) |-> a) is TRUE and vacuous at every attempt; the and
# is TRUE exactly when b holds, nonvacuous through b (a sequence is always nonvacuous).
property: ($isunknown(c) |-> a) and b
clause: 16.14.8 20.9
class: implementation-defined
origin: found while checking the X-check skip criterion
kind: cover
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1 2
a       : . . 1
b       : 1 . 1
c       : 1 1 1
outcome : T F T
decided : 0 1 2
pass    : 1 . 1
pending: 0

== isunknown_false_sequence_operand_makes_or_nonvacuous
# 16.14.8 (p or q is nonvacuous when either operand is), 20.9 (two-state model, as above).
# $isunknown(c) or ($isunknown(c) |-> a): the implication is TRUE
# vacuously at the attempt's start, so the or is TRUE there, and nonvacuous through its
# sequence operand (FALSE, but a sequence is always nonvacuous): covered at every sample.
property: $isunknown(c) or ($isunknown(c) |-> a)
clause: 16.14.8 20.9
class: implementation-defined
origin: as above
kind: cover
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1
a       : . 1
c       : 1 1
outcome : T T
decided : 0 1
pass    : 1 1
pending: 0

== throughout_over_a_nullable_operand_keeps_the_empty_match
# 16.9.9 (throughout), 16.9.2.1 (empty match: (empty ##1 s) is ##0 s), 20.9 (two-state model).
# The directive was skipped as "can never fail".
# $isunknown(a) is 0, so ($isunknown(a) throughout a[*0:1]) has only its empty match, and
# (empty ##1 1'b1) matches 1'b1 at the attempt's own sample: the antecedent matches at every
# sample, and the assertion fails wherever b is low.
property: (($isunknown(a) throughout a[*0:1]) ##1 1'b1) |-> b
clause: 16.9.9 16.9.2.1 20.9
class: implementation-defined
origin: a review (P1)
sample : 0 1 2
a      : 1 1 .
b      : 1 . 1
fail   : . 1 .
pass   : 1 . 1
pending: 0

== goto_of_a_constant_true_is_decided_at_its_own_samples_disabled
# Annex F (an unknown future sample satisfies every Boolean), 16.9.2 (goto), 16.12
# (disable iff).
# Pruning the constant letters of 1'b1[->2] emptied the automaton and
# the monitor failed at sample 0; superseded by the rule that constants are evaluated at their own sample.
# 1'b1[->2] matches exactly over samples 0..1 (its !1'b1 letters are false at known samples);
# a ##[2:$] b cannot end before sample 2, so the intersect is decided FALSE at sample 1, where
# d disables the attempt. Attempts 1 and 2 are disabled at their start: no failure at all.
property: 1'b1[->2] intersect (a ##[2:$] b)
clause: 16.9.2 16.9.6 16.12 Annex F
class: interpretation I1
origin: a review (P1)
disable: d
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1 2
a       : 1 1 .
b       : . . .
d       : . 1 1
outcome : d d d
decided : 1 1 2
pending: 0

== goto_of_a_constant_true_is_decided_at_its_own_samples
# As above without the disable: attempt 0 fails at sample 1, not at sample 0. Attempt 1 is
# still pending at the end (1'b1[->2] from sample 1 may end at 3 or later through an unknown
# !1'b1 sample, and a ##[2:$] b from sample 1 ends at 3 or later).
property: 1'b1[->2] intersect (a ##[2:$] b)
clause: 16.9.2 16.9.6 Annex F
class: interpretation I1
origin: a review (P1), as above
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1
a       : 1 1
b       : . .
outcome : F p
decided : 1 .
fail    : . 1
pending: 1
final-fail: 0

== cover_of_an_implication_whose_antecedent_cannot_match
# 16.9.6 (intersect: both operands match over the same interval), 16.14.8.
# A cover with an impossible antecedent is accepted and
# never covered (its attempts are vacuous). a intersect (b ##1 c): lengths 1 and 2 never meet.
property: (a intersect (b ##1 c)) |-> d
clause: 16.9.6 16.14.8
class: normative
origin: a review (low)
kind: cover
sample : 0 1 2
a      : 1 1 1
b      : 1 1 1
c      : 1 1 1
d      : 1 1 1
pending: 0

== goto_of_a_past_condition_skips_a_definitely_false_first_sample
# 16.9.2 (b[->1] is !b[*0:$] ##1 b), 16.9.3 ($past is X at the first sample), 11.4.7
# (0 && X is 0).
# CE-04: the goto's generated negated
# condition !(a && $past(b)) was gated false at the first sample, so attempt 0 died there and
# the monitor never failed. Attempt 0: x holds; at sample 0 a && $past(b) is 0 && X = 0, so
# the goto skips it; at sample 1 it is 1 && b@0 = 1, the antecedent matches and the
# consequent 1'b0 fails: FALSE at 1, nonvacuous. Attempts 1 and 2: x is low, vacuous TRUE.
# The pipeline rejects the property (the condition uses $past and could be known at the first
# sample); the table is checked by the reference evaluator.
property: x ##0 (a && $past(b))[->1] |-> 1'b0
clause: 16.9.2 16.9.3 11.4.7
class: normative
origin: a review (CE-04)
support: oracle
rejected-by: could be known true at the first sample
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1 2
x       : 1 . .
a       : . 1 .
b       : 1 1 .
outcome : F t t
decided : 1 1 2
fail    : . 1 .
pending: 0

== nonconsecutive_of_a_past_condition_skips_a_definitely_false_first_sample
# 16.9.2 (b[=1] is !b[*0:$] ##1 b ##1 !b[*0:$]), 16.9.3, 11.4.7.
# CE-04: the same defect for nonconsecutive repetition. The first match of
# attempt 0 ends at sample 1 (as above), where the consequent fails: FALSE at 1.
property: x ##0 (a && $past(b))[=1] |-> 1'b0
clause: 16.9.2 16.9.3 11.4.7
class: normative
origin: a review (CE-04), the same defect for nonconsecutive repetition
support: oracle
rejected-by: could be known true at the first sample
scope: attempt
sample  : 0 1 2
x       : 1 . .
a       : . 1 .
b       : 1 1 .
outcome : F t t
decided : 1 1 2
fail    : . 1 .
pending: 0

== signed_operand_zero_extended_in_an_unsigned_sum
# 11.8.2: signed'(s) is a signed 4-bit value, v is unsigned 8-bit, so the sum is an unsigned
# 8-bit context and the signed operand is zero-extended (not sign-extended).
# Sample 0: s = 15 (1111, signed -1), v = 1: 15 + 1 = 16 in 8 bits, equal: pass.
# (A sign-extended 0xFF + 1 would be 0 and the assertion would fail.)
# Sample 1: s = 15, v = 2: 15 + 2 = 17 != 16: fail. Sample 2: a is 0, vacuous.
property: a |-> (signed'(s) + v) == 8'd16
clause: 11.8.2
class: normative
origin: an implementation (pre-existing Convert sign extension)
signals: a s:4 v:8
sample : 0 1 2
a      : 1 1 .
s      : 15 15 .
v      : 1 2 .
fail   : . 1 .
pass   : 1 . .
